# Copy to deploy/auth.env and `chmod 600` it. Gitignored.
#
# Handed to the container by env_file, so every value is passed through
# LITERALLY — no ${...} substitution. That is why the hash lives here and not
# in .env, where compose would expand the $-separated fields of an scrypt hash
# into nothing.

# Produce this with:
#
#   docker compose run --rm kinopub-gui -hash-password
#
# It prompts twice with echo off and prints the line to paste. Nothing is saved
# by that command — it runs inside the container, where this file is not even
# mounted. Paste with an editor rather than a shell redirect, so the hash stays
# out of your shell history. Twelve characters minimum.
#
# Setting this turns the login on for EVERY request, the LAN included. Removing
# it turns the login off — which is why KINOPUB_PUBLIC_HOST in .env has to go
# with it: the server refuses to start with a public hostname and no password.
KINOPUB_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH=

# The account name the login form must match. Optional; defaults to "admin".
# Still ONE account: a wrong name and a wrong password give the same answer, so
# it is a real check rather than a way to enumerate users.
#KINOPUB_AUTH_USER=admin

# The second factor normally lives in /config/totp.json, enrolled from
# Settings → Security once you are signed in — a button, not a file, because
# only someone already inside can add one. This variable is the headless
# alternative and WINS if set; the page then says so instead of offering a
# disable button it could not honour. A malformed value here is a startup
# error, same as a malformed password hash.
#KINOPUB_AUTH_TOTP_SECRET=
